\subsection{Discussion of Experiment 2}

For the three completed algorithms, increasing $K$ generally improves
PSNR, SSIM, uniformity and class separability because additional
thresholds permit a finer reconstruction of the MRI intensity
distribution. Objective choice has a pronounced effect: Otsu and Kapur
produce stronger reconstruction metrics than Tsallis with the single
tested value $q=0.8$. At $K=12$, JADE records the highest mean Otsu PSNR
(38.1270~dB) and Kapur PSNR (37.0197~dB), while LADE records the highest
mean Tsallis PSNR (32.6407~dB), narrowly ahead of DE (32.5967~dB).
Across the 18 objective--$K$ cells, JADE has the highest descriptive
mean PSNR in nine cells, DE in six and LADE in three. These pooled means
describe the completed results but do not establish statistical
superiority. SHADE and L-SHADE must be completed on all 15 slices before
the planned five-algorithm paired tests and overall ranking are valid.

% 8. STATISTICAL ANALYSIS


\section{Statistical Analysis}
\label{sec:statistics}

Because stochastic evolutionary algorithms may produce different
solutions across independent runs, statistical tests are used to
determine whether observed performance differences are statistically
significant.

A significance level of

\begin{equation}
\alpha = 0.05
\end{equation}

is used.


\subsection{Wilcoxon Signed-Rank Test}

Within each dataset--objective--$K$ cell, the planned pairwise analysis
uses the median fitness over 30 runs for each image. Algorithm values are
therefore paired by image, and the two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test
tests the null hypothesis that the paired differences are centred at
zero. Holm correction controls the family-wise error rate across the
algorithm pairs within a cell. Rank-biserial correlation is reported as
an effect size, with its sign oriented so that a positive value favours
the first algorithm. Final values are withheld until the complete CHAOS
SHADE and L-SHADE grid is available.


\subsection{Friedman Test}

The planned omnibus comparison treats each image--$K$ combination as a
block and the five algorithms as treatments, using the median over the
30 runs for each block. The Friedman null hypothesis is that the
algorithms have equivalent ranks. The Iman--Davenport correction is used
for the omnibus significance decision, followed by a Nemenyi critical
difference comparison of mean ranks when appropriate. Incomplete blocks
are excluded; consequently, reporting this test before both CHAOS
algorithms finish would discard much of the experiment and is deferred.


